= Sub-Topic 3: Comparative Analysis: Permanent Settlement vs Ryotwari

To grasp how colonial land settlements transformed different regions of India, the comparative architecture below juxtaposes Bengal's Permanent Settlement with the Bombay Deccan's Ryotwari system:

#figure(
  image("assets/hist_colonial_settlement_process.png", width: 95%),
  caption: [Comparative Framework of Colonial Land Revenue Systems: Permanent Settlement vs Ryotwari],
)

== 3.1 Structural Contrast
+ *Legal Landlord*: In Bengal, the zamindar was the legal proprietor of the soil; in the Deccan, the individual peasant (*ryot*) was the registered occupant.
+ *Demand Permanence*: Bengal's demand was fixed in perpetuity; Deccan assessments were subject to upward revision every 30 years.
+ *Agrarian Intermediary*: Bengal preserved the zamindar and produced the rich *jotedar*; the Deccan bypassed landlords, creating a direct dependency between the peasant and the usurious village moneylender (*sahukar*).

