  [Confusing Jotedars with Zamindars],
  [Assuming Jotedars were absentee urban landlords.],
  [*Jotedars* were rich *resident* peasants living directly in villages. They controlled sharecroppers (*adhiyars*) and frequently subverted absentee zamindars.],
  
  [Assuming all Zamindaris collapsed in Bengal],
  [Believing the Permanent Settlement completely destroyed the old landed aristocracy.],
  [While many small estates were partitioned, great zamindars (like Burdwan) survived by using *benami* (fictitious) sales, transferring land to women, and deploying armed *lathyals*.],
  
  [Confusing Paharias and Santhals],
  [Matching Santhals with the hoe and Paharias with the plough.],
  [The *Paharias* practiced shifting cultivation using the *hoe* (*kural*). The *Santhals* practiced settled agriculture using the *plough*.],
  
  [Misinterpreting the 1859 Limitation Law],
  [Assuming the law reduced interest rates to 3%.],
  [The law made loan bonds legally valid for *three years*. Moneylenders circumvented it by compelling peasants to renew bonds every three years, compounding interest into principal.],
  
  [Misunderstanding Deccan Riots Commission Findings],
  [Assuming the Commission blamed the colonial government's high land tax.],
  [The Commission deliberately absolved the colonial revenue system of blame, attributing peasant destitution exclusively to greedy moneylenders.]
